sections.s2_technical:
First, understand what log compaction guarantees. A compacted topic, configured with `cleanup.policy=compact`, promises to retain at least the latest value for every key. It does not promise to remove old values immediately. The removal is done by a background component called the log cleaner, which periodically scans partitions and rewrites segments so that for each key only the most recent record survives. Because this is a background process, there is always a lag between when you write a new value or a tombstone and when the old values physically disappear.

Second, understand the active segment. A Kafka partition's log is a series of segment files, and the newest one is the active segment that is still being appended to. The log cleaner never compacts the active segment; it only works on the older, closed segments, which Kafka calls the dirty portion of the log once they accumulate enough updates. The decision to clean is driven partly by `min.cleanable.dirty.ratio`, which is the fraction of the log that must be stale before the cleaner bothers to run on that partition. So if your recent writes are still in the active segment, or the dirty ratio is below the threshold, old records simply have not been eligible for cleaning yet.

Third, understand tombstone retention. When you delete a key you send a record with that key and a null value. Compaction keeps this tombstone, not immediately discards it, because consumers that are behind need to actually read the tombstone to learn the key was deleted. The tombstone is retained for at least `delete.retention.ms`, which defaults to twenty-four hours. Only after that window, and after the next cleaning pass, is the tombstone itself removed. So for a period after deletion you can legitimately see the tombstone, and until the cleaner has processed the segments containing the old values, you can also still see those old values ahead of the tombstone in offset order.

Here is the producer code to delete a key in a compacted topic, which is just sending a null value:

```java
// Delete account SAV2001's cached profile from a compacted topic
producer.send(new ProducerRecord<>("account.profiles", "SAV2001", null));
// value == null is the tombstone; compaction will later drop older SAV2001 records
```

Several senior-level nuances follow. First, a consumer that starts from the earliest offset and reads the whole compacted log must be written to handle a null value as "delete this key from my local view"; if it ignores nulls it will keep a stale entry forever. Second, compaction is per partition, so the key's placement matters and the cleaner's progress differs across partitions. Third, you can tune responsiveness: lowering `segment.ms` or `segment.bytes` rolls the active segment sooner so recent updates become cleanable faster, lowering `min.cleanable.dirty.ratio` makes the cleaner run more aggressively, and lowering `delete.retention.ms` shortens how long tombstones linger, but setting it too low risks a slow consumer missing the delete entirely and keeping a stale key. Fourth, if the topic is configured with `cleanup.policy=compact,delete`, then normal time or size retention also applies on top of compaction, which interacts with tombstones in additional ways. Fifth, records with a null key cannot be compacted at all, because compaction is keyed, so a mistakenly null-keyed record will persist until ordinary retention removes it.

